WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.142
Logic is the structure that connects reasons to conclusions.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:05.847
A premise is a reason or supporting statement.

00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:09.227
A conclusion is what the argument is trying to establish.

00:00:09.227 --> 00:00:12.607
In everyday work, arguments are rarely written like formal proofs,

00:00:12.607 --> 00:00:14.635
but the structure is still there.

00:00:14.635 --> 00:00:17.678
Someone claims a project is behind because requirements changed,

00:00:17.678 --> 00:00:21.734
or that an alert is benign because similar alerts were false positives.

00:00:21.734 --> 00:00:25.452
Critical thinking asks whether the support actually leads to the conclusion.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:26.805
At a practical level,

00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:31.537
valid reasoning means the conclusion follows from the premises if the premises are true.

00:00:31.537 --> 00:00:35.593
Sound reasoning means the structure works and the premises are actually true.

00:00:35.593 --> 00:00:38.636
In real situations, you may not need formal labels,

00:00:38.636 --> 00:00:42.016
but you do need the habit of checking both parts.

00:00:42.016 --> 00:00:45.734
Does the argument make sense, and are the supporting facts reliable?

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:47.762
Cause and effect deserve special care.

00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:49.791
Correlation means two things vary together.

00:00:49.791 --> 00:00:52.833
Causation means one thing produces or contributes to another.

00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:57.227
If user complaints rise after a deployment, the deployment may be the cause,

00:00:57.227 --> 00:01:00.270
but it may also be one factor among several,

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.298
or the timing may be coincidental.

00:01:02.298 --> 00:01:06.016
Critical thinkers ask what mechanism connects the events, what alternatives exist,

00:01:06.016 --> 00:01:08.044
and what evidence would distinguish them.

00:01:08.044 --> 00:01:10.749
Necessary and sufficient conditions are another useful distinction.

00:01:10.749 --> 00:01:14.129
A necessary condition must be present for something to occur.

00:01:14.129 --> 00:01:17.847
A sufficient condition is enough to produce the result by itself.

00:01:17.847 --> 00:01:20.552
Confusing the two can lead to weak conclusions.

00:01:20.552 --> 00:01:23.594
For example, authentication is necessary for many secure systems,

00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:26.298
but authentication alone is not sufficient for security.

00:01:26.298 --> 00:01:27.650
Other controls still matter.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.031
Common argument problems include false dilemmas, circular reasoning, and overgeneralization.

00:01:31.031 --> 00:01:34.073
A false dilemma pretends there are only two choices.

00:01:34.073 --> 00:01:36.777
Circular reasoning assumes the conclusion inside the premise.

00:01:36.777 --> 00:01:38.805
Overgeneralization stretches limited evidence too far.

00:01:38.805 --> 00:01:41.848
Argument quality matters even when the conclusion sounds plausible,

00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:46.580
because weak reasoning can accidentally point to a true conclusion for the wrong reasons.
